 The current clang link does not combine libLLVM or libclang-cpp with its component archives. The recorded configuration has plugin support enabled, LLVM_EXPORT_SYMBOLS_FOR_PLUGINS disabled, and adds --export-dynamic to the statically linked clang. Exporting its symbols does not itself add another LLVM definition. This does not prove compatibility with an arbitrary plugin that brings its own LLVM copy; that case needs a plugin-specific loading check. The next validation will include a minimal plugin-loading case with the intended linking mode; no product change is proposed for this question.
 
-## Q6 — Scope of the BOLT switch
+## Q6 — Disabling the optimizations
 
-No: changing bcond_without clang_bolt to bcond_with disables Part 2 only. Hybrid linking and the fixed flags and archive policy remain enabled, so this does not restore the pre-PS3 tree or its complete build result. Restoring that result requires selecting the corresponding earlier revision or explicitly reverting the later parts.
+Done in PS5. The llvm_speedup switch is enabled by default. Setting it to disabled with --without llvm_speedup disables both hybrid linkage (Part 1) and the BOLT pipeline (Part 2), restoring the parent linking configuration and ordinary clang. The installed AddLLVM.cmake and AddClang.cmake texts still include the conditional support, so this does not restore the entire parent tree.
 
+Part 3 remains enabled to keep the fixed compilation flags and archive policy, including filtering project options that clang does not accept. Part 4 remains enabled because its converter checks protect input validity independently of either optimization.
+
+The independent switches are hybrid_link and clang_bolt: --without hybrid_link disables only hybrid linkage, and --without clang_bolt disables only BOLT. BOLT can remain enabled with hybrid linkage disabled. Disabling llvm_speedup overrides both individual switches.
